1
00:00:00,275 --> 00:00:05,838
In 1827, the botanist Robert Brown put grains of pollen in water, and looked

2
00:00:05,838 --> 00:00:09,325
at the tiny particles they released under his microscope.

3
00:00:09,325 --> 00:00:11,638
The particles never stopped moving.

4
00:00:11,638 --> 00:00:16,908
Each one jittered, darting this way and that, for no visible reason.

5
00:00:16,908 --> 00:00:20,258
And the jitter isn't a vibration around some home position.

6
00:00:20,258 --> 00:00:21,821
The particle wanders.

7
00:00:21,821 --> 00:00:23,071
Watch its trail:

8
00:00:23,071 --> 00:00:28,058
it never settles, and it never retraces a smooth curve.

9
00:00:28,242 --> 00:00:31,367
In 1905, Einstein explained it.

10
00:00:31,367 --> 00:00:36,092
The particle is being hit from every side by water molecules, and at any instant

11
00:00:36,092 --> 00:00:38,354
the kicks never quite cancel.

12
00:00:38,354 --> 00:00:42,917
The leftover push points somewhere new every moment.

13
00:00:43,075 --> 00:00:46,863
Release a crowd of such particles from one point, and they spread out like

14
00:00:46,863 --> 00:00:48,213
a drop of ink.

15
00:00:48,213 --> 00:00:50,850
Einstein's key prediction was how fast:

16
00:00:50,850 --> 00:00:54,963
the average squared distance grows in proportion to time, so the width of

17
00:00:54,963 --> 00:00:57,863
the cloud grows like the square root of time.

18
00:00:57,863 --> 00:01:02,125
A few years later, Jean Perrin measured exactly that, and used it to count

19
00:01:02,125 --> 00:01:04,550
molecules.

20
00:01:04,792 --> 00:01:09,054
Now follow just one coordinate of one particle as time goes by, and you get

21
00:01:09,054 --> 00:01:10,479
a graph like this.

22
00:01:10,479 --> 00:01:12,267
This is Brownian motion:

23
00:01:12,267 --> 00:01:15,792
the mathematician's idealized version of that jitter.

24
00:01:15,792 --> 00:01:20,892
Louis Bachelier used it in 1900 to model prices on the Paris stock exchange,

25
00:01:20,892 --> 00:01:24,254
and Norbert Wiener made it rigorous in the 1920s.

26
00:01:24,254 --> 00:01:28,854
Today it's the basic model of noise in physics, biology, engineering, and

27
00:01:28,854 --> 00:01:31,192
finance.

28
00:01:31,317 --> 00:01:33,529
And it breaks ordinary calculus.

29
00:01:33,529 --> 00:01:35,179
Here's a simple test.

30
00:01:35,179 --> 00:01:40,229
In ordinary calculus, the integral of s d s, from zero to x, is x squared

31
00:01:40,229 --> 00:01:41,392
over two.

32
00:01:41,392 --> 00:01:46,467
So for our path, the integral of W d W, from time zero to time one, ought

33
00:01:46,467 --> 00:01:50,200
to be W at time one, squared, over two.

34
00:01:50,200 --> 00:01:52,962
Let's just compute it, the way integrals are defined:

35
00:01:52,962 --> 00:01:58,025
chop time into steps, multiply W by its change over each step, and add up.

36
00:01:58,025 --> 00:02:00,500
We'll use more than four million steps.

37
00:02:00,500 --> 00:02:06,650
Our path ends at 1.2528, so half its square is 0.7847.

38
00:02:06,650 --> 00:02:10,112
But the sum comes out to 0.2847.

39
00:02:10,112 --> 00:02:13,200
We're off by 0.5000.

40
00:02:13,200 --> 00:02:15,050
Maybe that path was special.

41
00:02:15,050 --> 00:02:18,987
So here are five more, each with sixty-five thousand steps.

42
00:02:18,987 --> 00:02:19,937
The gaps:

43
00:02:19,937 --> 00:02:28,987
0.498, 0.501, 0.503, 0.499, 0.495.

44
00:02:28,987 --> 00:02:33,025
Every time, almost exactly one half.

45
00:02:33,208 --> 00:02:37,021
That missing half isn't rounding error, and it isn't bad luck.

46
00:02:37,021 --> 00:02:41,308
The true answer is one half W squared, minus one half.

47
00:02:41,308 --> 00:02:45,346
And that extra term is the first sign of a different kind of calculus:

48
00:02:45,346 --> 00:02:51,408
stochastic calculus, created by Kiyosi Itô in the 1940s.

49
00:02:51,542 --> 00:02:52,717
Here's the plan.

50
00:02:52,717 --> 00:02:56,404
First, Brownian motion itself, and the one strange fact that drives everything

51
00:02:56,404 --> 00:02:57,254
else:

52
00:02:57,254 --> 00:02:58,967
d W squared equals d t.

53
00:02:58,967 --> 00:03:02,104
Then the Itô integral, and where the missing half comes from.

54
00:03:02,104 --> 00:03:03,392
Then Itô's lemma:

55
00:03:03,392 --> 00:03:05,879
the chain rule, corrected for randomness.

56
00:03:05,879 --> 00:03:09,592
Then the equations of noise, and a surprising bridge between random paths

57
00:03:09,592 --> 00:03:12,192
and the partial differential equations of physics.

58
00:03:12,192 --> 00:03:13,942
And finally two payoffs:

59
00:03:13,942 --> 00:03:18,217
how Black and Scholes priced options by hedging the noise away, and how Girsanov's

60
00:03:18,217 --> 00:03:19,917
theorem changes the odds.

61
00:03:25,772 --> 00:03:29,947
Brownian motion is the limit of the simplest random process there is.

62
00:03:29,947 --> 00:03:31,434
Flip a fair coin.

63
00:03:31,434 --> 00:03:32,909
Heads, take a step up;

64
00:03:32,909 --> 00:03:34,560
tails, a step down.

65
00:03:34,560 --> 00:03:39,322
Do it again, and again, and you trace out a random walk.

66
00:03:39,490 --> 00:03:43,565
To get something in continuous time, we'll squeeze n steps into one unit of

67
00:03:43,565 --> 00:03:44,553
time.

68
00:03:44,553 --> 00:03:48,065
Each step takes a time delta t, equal to one over n.

69
00:03:48,065 --> 00:03:50,003
But how tall should each step be?

70
00:03:50,003 --> 00:03:52,632
Call the step size delta x.

71
00:03:52,632 --> 00:03:55,507
There's only one choice that works, and here's why.

72
00:03:55,507 --> 00:03:59,607
At time t, the walker's position is delta x times a sum of plus and minus

73
00:03:59,607 --> 00:04:02,132
ones, one for each step taken.

74
00:04:02,132 --> 00:04:05,507
The coin flips are independent, so their variances add:

75
00:04:05,507 --> 00:04:09,707
the number of steps, t over delta t, times the variance of each step, delta

76
00:04:09,707 --> 00:04:10,844
x squared.

77
00:04:10,844 --> 00:04:14,824
That's t, times delta x squared over delta t.

78
00:04:14,824 --> 00:04:17,111
So everything depends on that ratio.

79
00:04:17,111 --> 00:04:21,049
Make the steps as small as the time steps, and the variance dies away:

80
00:04:21,049 --> 00:04:22,411
the walk freezes.

81
00:04:22,411 --> 00:04:26,261
Make them bigger, like the fourth root, and the variance blows up.

82
00:04:26,261 --> 00:04:30,049
Only steps of size square root of delta t keep it finite:

83
00:04:30,049 --> 00:04:33,036
space has to scale like the square root of time.

84
00:04:33,036 --> 00:04:37,674
That square root is the seed of everything in this video.

85
00:04:37,790 --> 00:04:39,915
Here's that argument as a picture.

86
00:04:39,915 --> 00:04:42,390
Three panels, the same coin flips in each;

87
00:04:42,390 --> 00:04:44,128
only the step height differs.

88
00:04:44,128 --> 00:04:45,278
Now let n grow.

89
00:04:45,278 --> 00:04:46,653
Sixty-four steps.

90
00:04:46,653 --> 00:04:48,453
Two hundred and fifty-six.

91
00:04:48,453 --> 00:04:49,415
A thousand.

92
00:04:49,415 --> 00:04:50,665
Four thousand.

93
00:04:50,665 --> 00:04:54,415
With steps as small as delta t, the walk flattens to nothing.

94
00:04:54,415 --> 00:04:57,015
With the fourth root, it runs off the chart.

95
00:04:57,015 --> 00:05:00,490
But with the square root, the picture keeps the same overall size and just

96
00:05:00,490 --> 00:05:04,790
gets finer and finer, rougher and rougher.

97
00:05:04,915 --> 00:05:06,865
And what does the endpoint look like?

98
00:05:06,865 --> 00:05:11,353
At time one, it's a sum of n independent coin flips, divided by root n.

99
00:05:11,353 --> 00:05:16,253
The central limit theorem says that as n grows, that becomes a bell curve:

100
00:05:16,253 --> 00:05:20,115
a normal distribution with mean zero and variance one.

101
00:05:20,115 --> 00:05:23,790
Run many walks, and pile up where they end.

102
00:05:23,790 --> 00:05:25,078
Two hundred walks.

103
00:05:25,078 --> 00:05:25,903
Two thousand.

104
00:05:25,903 --> 00:05:26,965
Twenty thousand.

105
00:05:26,965 --> 00:05:30,015
And the bell curve emerges.

106
00:05:30,649 --> 00:05:35,286
Take the limit at every time, not just at the end, and you get Brownian motion,

107
00:05:35,286 --> 00:05:36,224
written W of t.

108
00:05:36,224 --> 00:05:38,299
Here are two hundred of its paths.

109
00:05:38,299 --> 00:05:43,036
At each time t, W of t is a bell curve with mean zero and variance t.

110
00:05:43,036 --> 00:05:46,236
So the spread of the paths grows like the square root of t:

111
00:05:46,236 --> 00:05:51,036
about two thirds stay within one root t, and ninety-five percent within two.

112
00:05:51,036 --> 00:05:56,399
The same square root law that Einstein found for the spreading cloud.

113
00:05:56,982 --> 00:05:59,469
Let's pin down exactly what we've built.

114
00:05:59,469 --> 00:06:01,682
Brownian motion starts at zero.

115
00:06:01,682 --> 00:06:05,382
What it does over one stretch of time is independent of what it did over any

116
00:06:05,382 --> 00:06:08,494
earlier stretch, just like fresh coin flips.

117
00:06:08,494 --> 00:06:12,857
Each increment, from time s to time t, is a bell curve with mean zero and

118
00:06:12,857 --> 00:06:14,557
variance t minus s:

119
00:06:14,557 --> 00:06:15,882
the elapsed time.

120
00:06:15,882 --> 00:06:18,044
And its paths are continuous:

121
00:06:18,044 --> 00:06:19,624
no jumps.

122
00:06:19,624 --> 00:06:24,249
Norbert Wiener proved in 1923 that a process with exactly these properties

123
00:06:24,249 --> 00:06:29,424
exists, and Monroe Donsker proved that rescaled random walks converge to it,

124
00:06:29,424 --> 00:06:34,761
whatever the step distribution, as long as it has mean zero and finite variance.

125
00:06:34,761 --> 00:06:37,336
So Brownian motion is universal:

126
00:06:37,336 --> 00:06:41,461
it's what any accumulation of many small, independent kicks looks like from

127
00:06:41,461 --> 00:06:42,399
far away.

128
00:06:44,567 --> 00:06:48,305
Brownian paths are continuous, but they're strange in another way.

129
00:06:48,305 --> 00:06:50,480
Here's an ordinary smooth curve.

130
00:06:50,480 --> 00:06:54,567
Let's zoom in on one point, by the same factor in both directions.

131
00:06:54,567 --> 00:06:58,580
Four times, sixteen, sixty-four, two hundred and fifty-six.

132
00:06:58,580 --> 00:07:02,130
The curve straightens out, until it's indistinguishable from a line:

133
00:07:02,130 --> 00:07:03,605
its tangent line.

134
00:07:03,605 --> 00:07:05,617
That's what having a derivative means:

135
00:07:05,617 --> 00:07:09,317
up close, the curve is a line.

136
00:07:09,817 --> 00:07:14,367
Now do the same to our Brownian path, zooming four times in both directions,

137
00:07:14,367 --> 00:07:16,005
again and again.

138
00:07:16,005 --> 00:07:19,217
Instead of straightening out, it gets steeper and wilder:

139
00:07:19,217 --> 00:07:22,305
the wiggles shoot off the top and bottom of the window.

140
00:07:22,305 --> 00:07:25,892
There is no tangent line to find.

141
00:07:26,334 --> 00:07:28,296
Here's the zoom that does work.

142
00:07:28,296 --> 00:07:32,309
Every time you stretch time by four, stretch space by only two:

143
00:07:32,309 --> 00:07:33,984
the square root of four.

144
00:07:33,984 --> 00:07:36,284
Now every level looks like the one before:

145
00:07:36,284 --> 00:07:40,234
just as rough, just as wild, statistically the same picture.

146
00:07:40,234 --> 00:07:44,559
These are all one path, simulated at four million points, and we could keep

147
00:07:44,559 --> 00:07:47,084
going forever.

148
00:07:48,292 --> 00:07:49,280
In symbols:

149
00:07:49,280 --> 00:07:53,667
Brownian motion with time sped up by a factor c has the same distribution

150
00:07:53,667 --> 00:07:56,130
as the original, scaled up by root c.

151
00:07:56,130 --> 00:07:57,392
It's a fractal.

152
00:07:57,392 --> 00:07:59,442
And that same square root again:

153
00:07:59,442 --> 00:08:03,542
zooming in never reveals anything smooth.

154
00:08:04,234 --> 00:08:06,496
This kills the derivative for good.

155
00:08:06,496 --> 00:08:09,384
A derivative is the limit of secant slopes:

156
00:08:09,384 --> 00:08:13,159
the change in W over a short time h, divided by h.

157
00:08:13,159 --> 00:08:17,609
But that change is a bell curve with variance h, so the slope is a bell curve

158
00:08:17,609 --> 00:08:19,259
with variance one over h.

159
00:08:19,259 --> 00:08:24,842
Its typical size is one over root h, and as h shrinks, it blows up.

160
00:08:24,842 --> 00:08:27,380
On our path, at the point we zoomed into:

161
00:08:27,380 --> 00:08:31,880
each time h shrinks by four, the slope roughly doubles, from about four, to

162
00:08:31,880 --> 00:08:35,730
eight, to over two hundred, right along with one over root h.

163
00:08:35,730 --> 00:08:41,517
Brownian motion is continuous everywhere, and differentiable nowhere.

164
00:08:42,134 --> 00:08:44,446
And there's an even stranger consequence.

165
00:08:44,446 --> 00:08:47,909
Walk along the path and add up the size of every move, up or down:

166
00:08:47,909 --> 00:08:49,709
the total distance traveled.

167
00:08:49,709 --> 00:08:52,784
Sampled at sixteen steps, our path travels 4.0.

168
00:08:52,784 --> 00:08:55,684
At 256 steps, 12.7.

169
00:08:55,684 --> 00:08:58,059
At four thousand, 50.6.

170
00:08:58,059 --> 00:09:01,121
And at four million steps, more than sixteen hundred.

171
00:09:01,121 --> 00:09:05,734
Each time the steps get sixteen times finer, the distance quadruples, exactly

172
00:09:05,734 --> 00:09:07,892
as the formula predicts.

173
00:09:07,892 --> 00:09:09,755
In the limit, it's infinite.

174
00:09:09,755 --> 00:09:14,792
A Brownian path has infinite length over every interval of time, however short.

175
00:09:14,792 --> 00:09:18,730
And that's a real problem for calculus, because the classical way to integrate

176
00:09:18,730 --> 00:09:23,042
against a curve, the Riemann–Stieltjes integral, needs the curve to have finite

177
00:09:23,042 --> 00:09:23,892
length.

178
00:09:23,892 --> 00:09:25,567
So we need a new idea.

179
00:09:25,567 --> 00:09:27,280
And it comes from a surprise:

180
00:09:27,280 --> 00:09:30,817
add up the squares of those moves instead, and the sum doesn't blow up at

181
00:09:30,817 --> 00:09:31,417
all.

182
00:09:33,365 --> 00:09:34,802
Here's that surprise.

183
00:09:34,802 --> 00:09:37,890
Take a smooth curve, and chop time into steps.

184
00:09:37,890 --> 00:09:42,352
On each step, build a square whose side is the curve's change over that step.

185
00:09:42,352 --> 00:09:45,340
Then pour all of the squares' area into this tank.

186
00:09:45,340 --> 00:09:48,315
Its capacity is exactly one unit of area:

187
00:09:48,315 --> 00:09:52,831
the same number as the length of our time interval, T equals one.

188
00:09:52,831 --> 00:09:56,331
For a smooth curve, the squares fill only part of the tank.

189
00:09:56,331 --> 00:09:59,469
And finer steps make it emptier and emptier:

190
00:09:59,469 --> 00:10:03,881
each change is about the slope times delta t, so each square is about delta

191
00:10:03,881 --> 00:10:08,131
t squared, and the n of them add up to something on the order of delta t,

192
00:10:08,131 --> 00:10:11,006
which goes to zero.

193
00:10:11,681 --> 00:10:13,194
Now the Brownian path.

194
00:10:13,194 --> 00:10:14,281
Sixteen steps:

195
00:10:14,281 --> 00:10:17,631
the squares more than fill the tank.

196
00:10:18,123 --> 00:10:19,298
Sixty-four steps:

197
00:10:19,298 --> 00:10:21,231
just over the brim.

198
00:10:21,231 --> 00:10:22,806
Two hundred and fifty-six:

199
00:10:22,806 --> 00:10:24,390
just under.

200
00:10:24,390 --> 00:10:25,227
Four thousand:

201
00:10:25,227 --> 00:10:27,040
0.993.

202
00:10:27,040 --> 00:10:27,952
Four million:

203
00:10:27,952 --> 00:10:28,790
1.0000.

204
00:10:28,790 --> 00:10:31,815
The squares' total area converges to exactly one:

205
00:10:31,815 --> 00:10:34,540
the elapsed time.

206
00:10:35,223 --> 00:10:35,773
Why?

207
00:10:35,773 --> 00:10:39,898
Each increment is a bell curve with variance delta t, so each square has an

208
00:10:39,898 --> 00:10:42,148
expected area of exactly delta t.

209
00:10:42,148 --> 00:10:46,123
Add up n of them, and the expected total is n delta t:

210
00:10:46,123 --> 00:10:48,565
the elapsed time, T.

211
00:10:48,565 --> 00:10:50,240
That's only the average.

212
00:10:50,240 --> 00:10:52,652
But the randomness disappears too.

213
00:10:52,652 --> 00:10:56,802
The variance of a single square works out to two delta t squared, using the

214
00:10:56,802 --> 00:11:00,402
fact that a standard bell curve has fourth moment three.

215
00:11:00,402 --> 00:11:05,352
The increments are independent, so the variances add, giving two T delta t.

216
00:11:05,352 --> 00:11:08,498
And that goes to zero as the steps shrink.

217
00:11:08,498 --> 00:11:12,923
A sum of many small, independent, positive pieces, whose fluctuations die

218
00:11:12,923 --> 00:11:13,635
away:

219
00:11:13,635 --> 00:11:15,585
it converges to a constant.

220
00:11:15,585 --> 00:11:17,023
Not just on average.

221
00:11:17,023 --> 00:11:20,248
On essentially every path.

222
00:11:20,798 --> 00:11:23,573
Here it is across four thousand fresh paths.

223
00:11:23,573 --> 00:11:27,223
With sixteen steps, the sum of squares is all over the place.

224
00:11:27,223 --> 00:11:30,323
With 256, it bunches up around one.

225
00:11:30,323 --> 00:11:33,073
With four thousand, it's nearly a spike.

226
00:11:33,073 --> 00:11:37,398
The spread shrinks exactly like root of two over n.

227
00:11:37,931 --> 00:11:40,769
The same is true at every moment along the way.

228
00:11:40,769 --> 00:11:44,731
Add up the squares only up to time t, and plot that running total.

229
00:11:44,731 --> 00:11:47,469
With sixteen steps it's a ragged staircase.

230
00:11:47,469 --> 00:11:48,794
Refine the steps:

231
00:11:48,794 --> 00:11:51,944
sixty-four, two fifty-six, four thousand.

232
00:11:51,944 --> 00:11:54,681
The staircase lies down on the line y equals t.

233
00:11:54,681 --> 00:11:59,206
The path is random, but its accumulated squared wiggle is a perfect clock:

234
00:11:59,206 --> 00:12:03,006
quadratic variation equals time.

235
00:12:04,665 --> 00:12:08,640
This is the one rule that makes stochastic calculus different, and we write

236
00:12:08,640 --> 00:12:10,190
it in a single line:

237
00:12:10,190 --> 00:12:12,365
d W squared equals d t.

238
00:12:12,365 --> 00:12:14,902
It doesn't mean each little square equals delta t.

239
00:12:14,902 --> 00:12:19,752
Each one is delta t times a random number with mean one, which can be almost

240
00:12:19,752 --> 00:12:22,065
zero or bigger than four.

241
00:12:22,065 --> 00:12:26,640
It means that in any sum over many small steps, the squares add up exactly

242
00:12:26,640 --> 00:12:28,673
like delta t would.

243
00:12:28,673 --> 00:12:31,385
And the other products of small quantities vanish.

244
00:12:31,385 --> 00:12:35,310
d t times d t sums to T delta t, which goes to zero.

245
00:12:35,310 --> 00:12:40,385
d t times d W sums to something like T root delta t, which also goes to zero.

246
00:12:40,385 --> 00:12:43,060
Only d W times d W survives.

247
00:12:43,060 --> 00:12:46,360
That's the multiplication table of stochastic calculus.

248
00:12:46,360 --> 00:12:50,898
In ordinary calculus, every product of two small changes is negligible.

249
00:12:50,898 --> 00:12:56,148
Here, d W is the size of root d t, so its square is as big as d t itself,

250
00:12:56,148 --> 00:12:57,098
and it stays.

251
00:13:02,977 --> 00:13:06,177
Most of science is written in differential equations:

252
00:13:06,177 --> 00:13:08,727
the rate of change of X is some function of X.

253
00:13:08,727 --> 00:13:11,702
To add randomness, it's natural to write:

254
00:13:11,702 --> 00:13:15,877
rate of change equals a drift, plus some amplitude times noise.

255
00:13:15,877 --> 00:13:17,277
But what is the noise?

256
00:13:17,277 --> 00:13:21,202
It would have to be the derivative of Brownian motion, and we just saw that

257
00:13:21,202 --> 00:13:22,927
doesn't exist.

258
00:13:22,927 --> 00:13:25,177
Here's what happens if you try anyway:

259
00:13:25,177 --> 00:13:29,777
the slopes of our path over steps of one sixty-fourth, one two-fifty-sixth,

260
00:13:29,777 --> 00:13:31,477
and one four-thousandth.

261
00:13:31,477 --> 00:13:34,202
The finer you look, the bigger the spikes.

262
00:13:34,202 --> 00:13:37,852
There's no function there to converge to.

263
00:13:38,127 --> 00:13:40,714
The way out is to never differentiate.

264
00:13:40,714 --> 00:13:42,939
Integrate both sides instead.

265
00:13:42,939 --> 00:13:47,464
X at time t is its starting value, plus the integral of the drift, which is

266
00:13:47,464 --> 00:13:51,127
an ordinary integral, plus the integral of sigma d W.

267
00:13:51,127 --> 00:13:52,964
That last one is new.

268
00:13:52,964 --> 00:13:57,064
To make sense of noisy differential equations, we first have to define what

269
00:13:57,064 --> 00:14:01,252
it means to integrate against a Brownian path.

270
00:14:01,769 --> 00:14:04,431
The cleanest way to think about it is gambling.

271
00:14:04,431 --> 00:14:08,669
Let W be the price of a stock, or your running score in a fair game.

272
00:14:08,669 --> 00:14:10,744
Let H be how many shares you hold:

273
00:14:10,744 --> 00:14:11,881
your stake.

274
00:14:11,881 --> 00:14:16,244
Over one short step, your gain is your stake times the change in the price,

275
00:14:16,244 --> 00:14:19,927
and your winnings, G, are the sum of all those gains.

276
00:14:19,927 --> 00:14:21,064
Here's a strategy:

277
00:14:21,064 --> 00:14:23,714
hold as many shares as the current price.

278
00:14:23,714 --> 00:14:25,102
Watch it play out.

279
00:14:25,102 --> 00:14:29,239
At the start of each step, you choose your stake, looking only at the past.

280
00:14:29,239 --> 00:14:33,639
Then the curtain lifts, the price moves, and the gain, stake times move, goes

281
00:14:33,639 --> 00:14:35,902
into your winnings.

282
00:14:35,902 --> 00:14:38,189
Notice the one rule we followed.

283
00:14:38,189 --> 00:14:40,727
Each stake was set before the price moved:

284
00:14:40,727 --> 00:14:44,364
you could see the past, but the future was behind a curtain.

285
00:14:44,364 --> 00:14:49,014
A strategy that never peeks is called adapted, or non-anticipating, and it's

286
00:14:49,014 --> 00:14:51,377
the only kind of integrand we allow.

287
00:14:51,377 --> 00:14:57,302
It's also the only kind a real gambler, or a real investor, can use.

288
00:14:58,735 --> 00:15:03,185
The Itô integral is what these winnings converge to as the steps shrink:

289
00:15:03,185 --> 00:15:07,185
the limit of the sum of H at the left end of each step, decided before the

290
00:15:07,185 --> 00:15:12,060
move, times the change in W across the step.

291
00:15:12,194 --> 00:15:16,069
Despite the path's infinite length, these sums do converge, because the random

292
00:15:16,069 --> 00:15:19,169
gains and losses cancel each other in a controlled way;

293
00:15:19,169 --> 00:15:21,356
we'll see exactly how in a moment.

294
00:15:21,356 --> 00:15:25,606
Here's the same strategy with sixty-four steps, two hundred and fifty-six,

295
00:15:25,606 --> 00:15:26,956
four thousand.

296
00:15:26,956 --> 00:15:31,069
The winnings settle onto one curve, and at time one they settle onto a number

297
00:15:31,069 --> 00:15:32,444
we've seen before:

298
00:15:32,444 --> 00:15:36,194
0.2847, the answer with the missing half.

299
00:15:38,322 --> 00:15:40,185
So where does the missing half come from?

300
00:15:40,185 --> 00:15:43,972
In ordinary calculus, it doesn't matter where inside each little step you

301
00:15:43,972 --> 00:15:45,472
evaluate the integrand:

302
00:15:45,472 --> 00:15:47,785
the left end, the right end, the middle.

303
00:15:47,785 --> 00:15:49,960
All the choices give the same limit.

304
00:15:49,960 --> 00:15:50,822
Not here.

305
00:15:50,822 --> 00:15:56,272
On our path, sampling W at the left end of each step gives 0.2847.

306
00:15:56,272 --> 00:15:59,885
Sampling at the right end gives 1.2847.

307
00:15:59,885 --> 00:16:03,522
The midpoint gives 0.7847.

308
00:16:03,522 --> 00:16:07,722
The right sum minus the left sum is the sum of W at the right end minus W

309
00:16:07,722 --> 00:16:10,510
at the left end, times delta W:

310
00:16:10,510 --> 00:16:13,672
that's the sum of the squares, delta W squared.

311
00:16:13,672 --> 00:16:15,347
And we know where that goes:

312
00:16:15,347 --> 00:16:17,447
to T, which here is one.

313
00:16:17,447 --> 00:16:20,947
And notice the midpoint gives exactly half of W squared:

314
00:16:20,947 --> 00:16:24,722
the answer from ordinary calculus.

315
00:16:25,255 --> 00:16:28,068
Here's the exact algebra, on one step.

316
00:16:28,068 --> 00:16:32,705
The change in W squared, from the left end to the right end, is W plus delta

317
00:16:32,705 --> 00:16:40,139
W, squared, minus W squared, which is two W delta W, plus delta W squared.

318
00:16:40,139 --> 00:16:42,364
Now add this up over all the steps.

319
00:16:42,364 --> 00:16:44,514
On the left, the sum telescopes:

320
00:16:44,514 --> 00:16:49,226
everything cancels except W squared at the end, minus W squared at the start,

321
00:16:49,226 --> 00:16:50,576
which is zero.

322
00:16:50,576 --> 00:16:51,626
On the right:

323
00:16:51,626 --> 00:16:55,489
twice the left-endpoint sum, plus the sum of the squares, which converges

324
00:16:55,489 --> 00:16:57,014
to T.

325
00:16:57,014 --> 00:17:02,676
In the limit, W squared at time T equals twice the integral of W d W, plus

326
00:17:02,676 --> 00:17:03,251
T.

327
00:17:03,251 --> 00:17:07,189
So the integral is one half W squared, minus one half T.

328
00:17:07,189 --> 00:17:13,239
That's the missing half, and it is the quadratic variation, in disguise.

329
00:17:13,864 --> 00:17:16,201
There's a lovely picture behind this.

330
00:17:16,201 --> 00:17:21,476
Draw a plane where both axes measure the value of W, and draw the line y equals

331
00:17:21,476 --> 00:17:21,939
w.

332
00:17:21,939 --> 00:17:27,322
As time runs, W moves back and forth along the horizontal axis.

333
00:17:27,322 --> 00:17:31,510
Take one step, from W at the left end to W at the right end.

334
00:17:31,510 --> 00:17:36,797
The left-endpoint gain, W times delta W, is the area of this rectangle:

335
00:17:36,797 --> 00:17:38,810
width delta W, height W.

336
00:17:38,810 --> 00:17:43,060
The area under the line over the same stretch is one half the new W squared,

337
00:17:43,060 --> 00:17:45,035
minus one half the old one.

338
00:17:45,035 --> 00:17:49,922
They differ by a triangle, with two legs of length delta W, and area one half

339
00:17:49,922 --> 00:17:52,089
delta W squared.

340
00:17:52,089 --> 00:17:56,589
And when the step goes the other way, the rectangle counts as negative area,

341
00:17:56,589 --> 00:18:00,701
overshoots the region under the line, and again they differ by exactly the

342
00:18:00,701 --> 00:18:02,551
same kind of triangle.

343
00:18:02,551 --> 00:18:09,039
Up or down, the left-endpoint sum always loses one half delta W squared.

344
00:18:10,539 --> 00:18:15,276
Now run all sixteen steps, and collect the triangles in a tank whose brim

345
00:18:15,276 --> 00:18:16,464
is at one half T.

346
00:18:16,464 --> 00:18:21,364
The areas under the line telescope to one half W squared at the end, no matter

347
00:18:21,364 --> 00:18:22,914
how the path wandered.

348
00:18:22,914 --> 00:18:26,964
But every step drops a triangle, and they pile up.

349
00:18:26,964 --> 00:18:31,814
With sixteen steps, the triangles hold 0.64, more than the brim.

350
00:18:31,814 --> 00:18:35,464
With 256 steps, 0.487.

351
00:18:35,464 --> 00:18:36,664
With four million:

352
00:18:36,664 --> 00:18:38,214
0.5000.

353
00:18:38,214 --> 00:18:43,126
For a smooth path, these triangles are second-order small and add up to nothing.

354
00:18:43,126 --> 00:18:46,726
For a Brownian path there are so many of them that they add up to exactly

355
00:18:46,726 --> 00:18:49,689
half the elapsed time.

356
00:18:50,322 --> 00:18:51,910
So which choice is right?

357
00:18:51,910 --> 00:18:56,522
Itô's left-endpoint integral gives one half W squared minus one half T.

358
00:18:56,522 --> 00:19:01,397
The midpoint gives the ordinary-calculus answer, one half W squared.

359
00:19:01,397 --> 00:19:03,685
It's called the Stratonovich integral.

360
00:19:03,685 --> 00:19:07,314
The right endpoint overshoots by one half T.

361
00:19:07,314 --> 00:19:09,114
Think about the gambler again.

362
00:19:09,114 --> 00:19:12,364
The midpoint and right-end rules set your stake using the price after the

363
00:19:12,364 --> 00:19:13,039
move:

364
00:19:13,039 --> 00:19:14,489
they peek at the future.

365
00:19:14,489 --> 00:19:16,401
And it shows in the averages.

366
00:19:16,401 --> 00:19:21,089
Since W squared has mean T, Itô's answer has mean zero, while the others win

367
00:19:21,089 --> 00:19:23,901
money on average, in a perfectly fair game.

368
00:19:23,901 --> 00:19:25,601
Only cheating does that.

369
00:19:25,601 --> 00:19:27,926
The left endpoint is the honest choice:

370
00:19:27,926 --> 00:19:31,522
Itô integrals are fair games, with mean zero.

371
00:19:31,522 --> 00:19:34,122
Stratonovich's integral isn't wrong, though.

372
00:19:34,122 --> 00:19:37,985
It's a different integral, and in physics it's often the natural one:

373
00:19:37,985 --> 00:19:42,672
if the noise is a limit of smooth, rapidly varying forces, the Stratonovich

374
00:19:42,672 --> 00:19:44,560
integral is what you get.

375
00:19:44,560 --> 00:19:47,510
The two are related by a simple correction term.

376
00:19:47,510 --> 00:19:52,597
In this video, we'll use Itô's, which is the standard in probability and finance.

377
00:19:54,642 --> 00:19:58,892
That fairness is the first of two rules that make the Itô integral work.

378
00:19:58,892 --> 00:20:03,392
On each step, the stake is fixed before the move, and the move is a fresh,

379
00:20:03,392 --> 00:20:06,230
independent coin flip with mean zero.

380
00:20:06,230 --> 00:20:11,417
So the expected gain on every step factors into the expected stake times zero.

381
00:20:11,417 --> 00:20:12,430
Add them up:

382
00:20:12,430 --> 00:20:16,075
every Itô integral has expected value zero.

383
00:20:16,075 --> 00:20:19,525
Here are our winnings from the double-down strategy, on two hundred different

384
00:20:19,525 --> 00:20:20,925
Brownian paths.

385
00:20:20,925 --> 00:20:25,088
They spread out, and some win big, but their average stays pinned at zero,

386
00:20:25,088 --> 00:20:26,563
all the way along.

387
00:20:26,563 --> 00:20:31,013
A process like this, whose best forecast of the future is its present value,

388
00:20:31,013 --> 00:20:32,688
is called a martingale.

389
00:20:32,688 --> 00:20:36,075
Itô integrals of adapted strategies are martingales:

390
00:20:36,075 --> 00:20:39,550
no strategy beats a fair game.

391
00:20:40,050 --> 00:20:43,275
The second rule tells us how big an Itô integral is.

392
00:20:43,275 --> 00:20:45,950
Square the sum, and take the average.

393
00:20:45,950 --> 00:20:50,413
Squaring a sum of n terms gives n squared cross terms, one for every pair

394
00:20:50,413 --> 00:20:51,388
of steps.

395
00:20:51,388 --> 00:20:53,450
Here they are as a grid.

396
00:20:53,450 --> 00:20:57,100
Take any cell off the diagonal, say steps two and five.

397
00:20:57,100 --> 00:20:59,288
Step five's move comes last.

398
00:20:59,288 --> 00:21:03,100
Everything else in the product is known by the start of step five, and step

399
00:21:03,100 --> 00:21:05,975
five's move is fresh, with mean zero.

400
00:21:05,975 --> 00:21:08,300
So the whole term averages to zero.

401
00:21:08,300 --> 00:21:11,225
The same goes for every off-diagonal cell.

402
00:21:11,225 --> 00:21:14,900
The fairness of each step wipes out all the cross terms.

403
00:21:14,900 --> 00:21:17,025
Only the diagonal survives:

404
00:21:17,025 --> 00:21:21,000
the stake squared times the move squared, which averages to the stake squared

405
00:21:21,000 --> 00:21:22,150
times delta t.

406
00:21:22,150 --> 00:21:25,750
Add up the diagonal, and you get an ordinary integral.

407
00:21:25,750 --> 00:21:27,975
This is the Itô isometry:

408
00:21:27,975 --> 00:21:31,850
the average square of the integral equals the average of the integral of H

409
00:21:31,850 --> 00:21:33,850
squared d t.

410
00:21:33,850 --> 00:21:39,675
Notice the d W squared equals d t rule hiding inside it, on the diagonal.

411
00:21:39,675 --> 00:21:44,038
And this identity is also what guarantees that the Riemann sums converge in

412
00:21:44,038 --> 00:21:45,363
the first place:

413
00:21:45,363 --> 00:21:51,725
it turns questions about random sums into questions about ordinary integrals.

414
00:21:52,200 --> 00:21:56,388
Let's test both rules on our double-down strategy, H equals W.

415
00:21:56,388 --> 00:22:00,350
The isometry predicts that the average square of the winnings is the integral

416
00:22:00,350 --> 00:22:02,963
of t d t from zero to one:

417
00:22:02,963 --> 00:22:04,559
one half.

418
00:22:04,559 --> 00:22:06,884
Now simulate forty thousand paths.

419
00:22:06,884 --> 00:22:08,296
The average of the winnings:

420
00:22:08,296 --> 00:22:12,534
minus 0.0007, zero up to sampling noise.

421
00:22:12,534 --> 00:22:13,471
The variance:

422
00:22:13,471 --> 00:22:15,246
0.505.

423
00:22:15,246 --> 00:22:16,709
Both rules check out.

424
00:22:16,709 --> 00:22:17,634
And look at the shape:

425
00:22:17,634 --> 00:22:20,934
you can never lose more than about one half, but you can win big.

426
00:22:20,934 --> 00:22:22,771
Lopsided, but fair.

427
00:22:22,771 --> 00:22:27,134
The ordinary-calculus answer, one half W squared, would average one half:

428
00:22:27,134 --> 00:22:30,734
it would win money in a fair game, which is exactly why it can't be right.

429
00:22:36,498 --> 00:22:40,173
In ordinary calculus, the chain rule tells you how a function of a changing

430
00:22:40,173 --> 00:22:41,648
quantity changes:

431
00:22:41,648 --> 00:22:43,861
d f equals f prime times d x.

432
00:22:43,861 --> 00:22:48,561
Applied to W squared, it would say the change in W squared is two W d W.

433
00:22:48,561 --> 00:22:50,461
But we've already found the truth:

434
00:22:50,461 --> 00:22:55,336
W squared at time T is twice the integral of W d W, plus T.

435
00:22:55,336 --> 00:22:58,386
So the change in W squared has an extra piece:

436
00:22:58,386 --> 00:22:59,448
plus d t.

437
00:22:59,448 --> 00:23:03,973
Where does it come from, and what is it for a general function?

438
00:23:04,406 --> 00:23:05,844
Go back to basics:

439
00:23:05,844 --> 00:23:07,269
Taylor's theorem.

440
00:23:07,269 --> 00:23:12,669
Over one small step, the change in f is f prime times delta W, plus one half

441
00:23:12,669 --> 00:23:17,819
f double prime times delta W squared, plus a cubic term, and so on.

442
00:23:17,819 --> 00:23:21,894
In ordinary calculus, only the first term matters, because the others are

443
00:23:21,894 --> 00:23:24,706
too small to survive being added up.

444
00:23:24,706 --> 00:23:28,169
But now delta W is the size of root delta t.

445
00:23:28,169 --> 00:23:33,419
So the first term is of order root delta t, the second of order delta t, and

446
00:23:33,419 --> 00:23:37,073
the third of order delta t to the three halves.

447
00:23:37,073 --> 00:23:40,673
Now add up n of them, one per step, where n is T over delta t.

448
00:23:40,673 --> 00:23:44,873
The first terms have random signs, and their sum is an Itô integral.

449
00:23:44,873 --> 00:23:48,123
The second terms are n times something of order delta t:

450
00:23:48,123 --> 00:23:49,711
that stays finite.

451
00:23:49,711 --> 00:23:54,486
In fact, by our rule, delta W squared adds up like delta t, so this sum becomes

452
00:23:54,486 --> 00:23:57,761
an ordinary integral of one half f double prime.

453
00:23:57,761 --> 00:24:01,798
The third terms add up to n times delta t to the three halves, which goes

454
00:24:01,798 --> 00:24:02,761
to zero.

455
00:24:02,761 --> 00:24:04,731
So do all the rest.

456
00:24:04,731 --> 00:24:06,031
Let's watch it happen.

457
00:24:06,031 --> 00:24:10,144
For f equals x cubed, Taylor's expansion stops after three terms, so it's

458
00:24:10,144 --> 00:24:11,169
exact:

459
00:24:11,169 --> 00:24:16,231
W cubed at time one is exactly the sum of these three columns, at every resolution.

460
00:24:16,231 --> 00:24:19,631
As the steps shrink, the cubic column dies away to nothing.

461
00:24:19,631 --> 00:24:24,681
The middle column settles to 0.338, which is three times the integral of W

462
00:24:24,681 --> 00:24:26,831
d t, just as predicted.

463
00:24:26,831 --> 00:24:28,544
And the totals never change:

464
00:24:28,544 --> 00:24:33,031
always W cubed, 1.966.

465
00:24:33,565 --> 00:24:35,815
So here's the corrected chain rule.

466
00:24:35,815 --> 00:24:41,390
The change in f of W is f prime d W, the term you'd expect, plus one half

467
00:24:41,390 --> 00:24:43,302
f double prime d t:

468
00:24:43,302 --> 00:24:46,990
a new term that comes straight from d W squared equals d t.

469
00:24:46,990 --> 00:24:49,073
This is Itô's lemma.

470
00:24:49,073 --> 00:24:50,673
Check it on W squared:

471
00:24:50,673 --> 00:24:55,023
f double prime is two, so the change is two W d W plus d t.

472
00:24:55,023 --> 00:24:59,636
Exactly what the telescoping sum told us, by a completely different route.

473
00:24:59,636 --> 00:25:04,373
For W cubed, three W squared d W plus three W d t:

474
00:25:04,373 --> 00:25:06,348
the two columns in the table.

475
00:25:06,348 --> 00:25:12,348
And for e to the W, the correction is one half e to the W d t.

476
00:25:12,731 --> 00:25:14,881
Here's the lemma at work on our path.

477
00:25:14,881 --> 00:25:17,969
The faint thick curve is W squared itself.

478
00:25:17,969 --> 00:25:23,594
The red curve integrates the ordinary chain rule, two W d W, step by step:

479
00:25:23,594 --> 00:25:26,056
it falls further and further behind.

480
00:25:26,056 --> 00:25:31,456
Add Itô's correction, plus t, and it lands right on top.

481
00:25:32,115 --> 00:25:33,552
Here's the intuition.

482
00:25:33,552 --> 00:25:35,940
Picture a curved bowl, f, and a point at x.

483
00:25:35,940 --> 00:25:39,752
The noise pushes x to the left or right by the same amount, h, with equal

484
00:25:39,752 --> 00:25:40,590
odds.

485
00:25:40,590 --> 00:25:42,690
On average, x hasn't moved.

486
00:25:42,690 --> 00:25:43,790
But f has:

487
00:25:43,790 --> 00:25:47,715
the average of the two outcomes is the midpoint of this chord, and on a convex

488
00:25:47,715 --> 00:25:50,165
curve, the chord lies above the curve.

489
00:25:50,165 --> 00:25:53,515
It sits higher by one half f double prime h squared.

490
00:25:53,515 --> 00:25:56,136
And h squared is delta t.

491
00:25:56,136 --> 00:25:58,823
So noise, plus curvature, makes drift.

492
00:25:58,823 --> 00:26:03,248
Across two hundred paths, the average of W stays at zero, but the average

493
00:26:03,248 --> 00:26:05,486
of W squared climbs steadily:

494
00:26:05,486 --> 00:26:07,811
one unit of height per unit of time.

495
00:26:07,811 --> 00:26:13,461
That climb is the Itô term, one half f double prime d t.

496
00:26:13,890 --> 00:26:17,202
The same argument works for anything driven by noise.

497
00:26:17,202 --> 00:26:21,465
Suppose X moves with a drift mu and a noise amplitude sigma:

498
00:26:21,465 --> 00:26:24,415
d X equals mu d t plus sigma d W.

499
00:26:24,415 --> 00:26:28,590
For a function of time and X, Taylor's theorem gives three terms that might

500
00:26:28,590 --> 00:26:29,690
survive:

501
00:26:29,690 --> 00:26:34,277
the time derivative, the slope times d X, and one half the curvature times

502
00:26:34,277 --> 00:26:36,186
d X squared.

503
00:26:36,186 --> 00:26:39,311
Use the multiplication table on d X squared.

504
00:26:39,311 --> 00:26:41,586
It expands into three products.

505
00:26:41,586 --> 00:26:46,961
d t squared is zero, d t d W is zero, and d W squared is d t.

506
00:26:46,961 --> 00:26:50,711
So d X squared is just sigma squared d t.

507
00:26:50,711 --> 00:26:52,111
Collect the terms:

508
00:26:52,111 --> 00:26:58,386
d f has a drift part, f t plus mu f x plus one half sigma squared f x x, all

509
00:26:58,386 --> 00:27:02,236
times d t, and a noise part, sigma f x d W.

510
00:27:02,236 --> 00:27:06,386
This is Itô's lemma in its general form, and it's the workhorse of everything

511
00:27:06,386 --> 00:27:07,386
that follows.

512
00:27:09,236 --> 00:27:12,299
Itô's lemma has consequences you can feel in your wallet.

513
00:27:12,299 --> 00:27:13,436
Here's a game.

514
00:27:13,436 --> 00:27:16,224
You start with a hundred dollars and flip a coin.

515
00:27:16,224 --> 00:27:18,999
Heads, your wealth grows by fifty percent.

516
00:27:18,999 --> 00:27:21,511
Tails, it shrinks by forty percent.

517
00:27:21,511 --> 00:27:25,961
On average, each round multiplies your money by 1.05:

518
00:27:25,961 --> 00:27:28,886
an expected gain of five percent per round.

519
00:27:28,886 --> 00:27:30,469
Should you play?

520
00:27:30,469 --> 00:27:34,194
Here's one player over a hundred rounds, on a logarithmic scale.

521
00:27:34,194 --> 00:27:37,669
And here are eighty more, out of ten thousand we simulated.

522
00:27:37,669 --> 00:27:42,319
The expected wealth really does grow by five percent a round, to 131 and a

523
00:27:42,319 --> 00:27:45,044
half times the stake after a hundred rounds.

524
00:27:45,044 --> 00:27:48,982
But the typical player goes down, and down, and down.

525
00:27:48,982 --> 00:27:52,469
After a hundred rounds, the median player has half a percent of their money

526
00:27:52,469 --> 00:27:56,478
left, and 87 percent of the players lost money.

527
00:27:56,478 --> 00:28:00,978
The average is held up by a handful of spectacularly lucky players, so rare

528
00:28:00,978 --> 00:28:05,203
that even the average of our ten thousand only reaches 52 times the stake,

529
00:28:05,203 --> 00:28:07,115
not 131.

530
00:28:07,115 --> 00:28:08,540
The reason is simple:

531
00:28:08,540 --> 00:28:15,340
one heads and one tails multiply your wealth by 1.5 times 0.6, which is 0.9.

532
00:28:15,340 --> 00:28:17,778
You lose ten percent every two rounds.

533
00:28:17,778 --> 00:28:21,840
For a typical path, what matters is the average of the logarithm, not the

534
00:28:21,840 --> 00:28:24,928
logarithm of the average.

535
00:28:25,553 --> 00:28:27,465
Now the continuous version.

536
00:28:27,465 --> 00:28:32,828
Let a price S have returns made of a drift, mu d t, plus noise, sigma d W.

537
00:28:32,828 --> 00:28:37,465
That's geometric Brownian motion, the standard model of a stock price.

538
00:28:37,465 --> 00:28:42,453
To match the coin game, take mu equal to five percent and sigma equal to forty-five

539
00:28:42,453 --> 00:28:43,853
percent per round:

540
00:28:43,853 --> 00:28:47,736
the game's average return and its standard deviation.

541
00:28:47,736 --> 00:28:50,411
Ordinary calculus suggests a solution:

542
00:28:50,411 --> 00:28:54,086
S equals S zero times e to the mu t plus sigma W.

543
00:28:54,086 --> 00:28:56,249
But check it with Itô's lemma.

544
00:28:56,249 --> 00:29:01,224
The function e to the mu t plus sigma w has time derivative mu f, slope sigma

545
00:29:01,224 --> 00:29:03,449
f, and curvature sigma squared f.

546
00:29:03,449 --> 00:29:09,061
So its change is S times mu d t, plus sigma d W, plus one half sigma squared

547
00:29:09,061 --> 00:29:09,749
d t.

548
00:29:09,749 --> 00:29:14,386
That extra term means the guess solves a different equation.

549
00:29:14,911 --> 00:29:17,524
The way to solve it is to look at the logarithm.

550
00:29:17,524 --> 00:29:19,774
Apply Itô's lemma to log S:

551
00:29:19,774 --> 00:29:25,736
one over S, times d S, minus one half, one over S squared, times d S squared.

552
00:29:25,736 --> 00:29:30,761
d S squared is sigma squared S squared d t, by the multiplication table.

553
00:29:30,761 --> 00:29:36,399
So the change in log S is mu d t plus sigma d W, minus one half sigma squared

554
00:29:36,399 --> 00:29:36,924
d t.

555
00:29:36,924 --> 00:29:38,436
Constant coefficients:

556
00:29:38,436 --> 00:29:40,744
we can integrate directly.

557
00:29:40,744 --> 00:29:45,332
Log S grows at the rate mu minus one half sigma squared, plus sigma times

558
00:29:45,332 --> 00:29:45,982
W.

559
00:29:45,982 --> 00:29:51,582
And the price is S zero, times e to the mu minus one half sigma squared, t,

560
00:29:51,582 --> 00:29:52,844
plus sigma W.

561
00:29:52,844 --> 00:29:56,932
The one half sigma squared is Itô's correction, and it's exactly what the

562
00:29:56,932 --> 00:30:00,019
naive guess was missing.

563
00:30:00,569 --> 00:30:02,469
Let's check that numerically.

564
00:30:02,469 --> 00:30:06,969
Simulate the equation directly, one small step at a time, a million steps,

565
00:30:06,969 --> 00:30:09,732
with one fixed sequence of random kicks.

566
00:30:09,732 --> 00:30:13,457
The naive formula, driven by the same kicks, flies off:

567
00:30:13,457 --> 00:30:17,907
after a hundred rounds it's too big by a factor of about twenty-five thousand.

568
00:30:17,907 --> 00:30:22,569
Itô's formula sits right on top of the simulation.

569
00:30:23,228 --> 00:30:26,315
Now we can see the coin game's paradox in the formula.

570
00:30:26,315 --> 00:30:29,653
Here are geometric Brownian paths with the game's parameters.

571
00:30:29,653 --> 00:30:33,703
The expected price grows like e to the mu t, because the exponential of a

572
00:30:33,703 --> 00:30:36,378
bell curve is pulled up by its lucky tail.

573
00:30:36,378 --> 00:30:40,528
But the typical path, the median, grows at the rate mu minus one half sigma

574
00:30:40,528 --> 00:30:41,478
squared.

575
00:30:41,478 --> 00:30:46,078
With mu at five percent and sigma at forty-five percent, that's minus five

576
00:30:46,078 --> 00:30:50,553
point one percent per round, almost exactly the coin game's minus five point

577
00:30:50,553 --> 00:30:51,803
three.

578
00:30:51,803 --> 00:30:55,253
On the log scale, the final prices form a bell curve:

579
00:30:55,253 --> 00:30:57,640
the price itself is log-normal.

580
00:30:57,640 --> 00:31:01,553
Eighty-seven percent of paths end below where they started, even though the

581
00:31:01,553 --> 00:31:04,953
expected price has grown a hundred and fifty times.

582
00:31:04,953 --> 00:31:09,178
This is volatility drag, and it's Itô's correction term at work:

583
00:31:09,178 --> 00:31:13,803
noise doesn't just add uncertainty, it lowers the long-run growth rate, by

584
00:31:13,803 --> 00:31:15,828
exactly one half sigma squared.

585
00:31:21,574 --> 00:31:25,599
With the integral and Itô's lemma in hand, we can finally write down noisy

586
00:31:25,599 --> 00:31:28,362
differential equations, and solve them.

587
00:31:28,362 --> 00:31:32,924
A stochastic differential equation says that over each instant, X changes

588
00:31:32,924 --> 00:31:38,199
by a drift, a steady push that depends on where X is, plus a random kick,

589
00:31:38,199 --> 00:31:41,533
whose size can also depend on where X is.

590
00:31:41,533 --> 00:31:44,745
Remember, it's shorthand for an integral equation:

591
00:31:44,745 --> 00:31:50,145
X at time t is where it started, plus the accumulated drift, plus an Itô integral

592
00:31:50,145 --> 00:31:52,533
of the noise.

593
00:31:52,966 --> 00:31:55,166
And we can simulate them directly.

594
00:31:55,166 --> 00:31:57,104
Step forward by delta t:

595
00:31:57,104 --> 00:32:02,041
add the drift times delta t, then add a random kick, the noise amplitude times

596
00:32:02,041 --> 00:32:05,204
root delta t times a fresh standard normal.

597
00:32:05,204 --> 00:32:06,266
Here's one step:

598
00:32:06,266 --> 00:32:08,379
the green arrow is the drift.

599
00:32:08,379 --> 00:32:10,766
The bell shows where the kick might land you.

600
00:32:10,766 --> 00:32:13,566
And a draw from it picks the next point.

601
00:32:13,566 --> 00:32:16,204
Repeat, and you've drawn a sample path.

602
00:32:16,204 --> 00:32:20,066
This is how nearly every simulation of a stochastic differential equation

603
00:32:20,066 --> 00:32:23,891
works, from molecules to markets.

604
00:32:24,299 --> 00:32:27,937
Here's the most important example after Brownian motion itself.

605
00:32:27,937 --> 00:32:30,087
The drift is minus theta X:

606
00:32:30,087 --> 00:32:33,924
always pulling back toward zero, harder the farther away you are.

607
00:32:33,924 --> 00:32:36,124
The green arrows show that pull.

608
00:32:36,124 --> 00:32:41,012
It's the Ornstein–Uhlenbeck process, invented in 1930 as the velocity of a

609
00:32:41,012 --> 00:32:43,637
Brownian particle slowed by friction.

610
00:32:43,637 --> 00:32:47,699
Today it also models interest rates, and anything that wanders but is pulled

611
00:32:47,699 --> 00:32:49,433
back to a mean.

612
00:32:49,433 --> 00:32:52,420
Start forty particles at two, and let them go.

613
00:32:52,420 --> 00:32:55,770
The drift pulls them in, and the noise keeps them jittering.

614
00:32:55,770 --> 00:33:01,333
They settle into a band around zero, where the pull and the kicks balance.

615
00:33:01,766 --> 00:33:06,141
And we can solve it exactly, with a trick from ordinary differential equations:

616
00:33:06,141 --> 00:33:07,941
an integrating factor.

617
00:33:07,941 --> 00:33:10,129
Look at e to the theta t, times X.

618
00:33:10,129 --> 00:33:14,629
Its time derivative is theta times itself, its slope in x is e to the theta

619
00:33:14,629 --> 00:33:17,529
t, and its curvature in x is zero:

620
00:33:17,529 --> 00:33:18,491
it's linear in x.

621
00:33:18,491 --> 00:33:22,641
So Itô's lemma gives the ordinary chain rule, with no correction, and the

622
00:33:22,641 --> 00:33:24,854
drift terms cancel exactly.

623
00:33:24,854 --> 00:33:27,133
Only the noise is left.

624
00:33:27,133 --> 00:33:29,170
Integrate, and multiply back:

625
00:33:29,170 --> 00:33:34,083
X at time t is the starting point, decayed by e to the minus theta t, plus

626
00:33:34,083 --> 00:33:39,270
a weighted Itô integral of the noise, where old kicks are forgotten exponentially.

627
00:33:39,270 --> 00:33:43,408
The Itô integral has mean zero, so the mean just decays.

628
00:33:43,408 --> 00:33:47,270
And the isometry gives the variance as an ordinary integral:

629
00:33:47,270 --> 00:33:51,983
sigma squared over two theta, times one minus e to the minus two theta t.

630
00:33:51,983 --> 00:33:56,833
As time goes on, every particle forgets where it started, and the distribution

631
00:33:56,833 --> 00:34:02,733
settles to a bell curve with variance sigma squared over two theta.

632
00:34:03,299 --> 00:34:07,237
Here's the prediction against three thousand simulated particles.

633
00:34:07,237 --> 00:34:12,399
As time runs, the histogram of where they are slides toward zero and widens,

634
00:34:12,399 --> 00:34:16,362
and the theoretical bell curve tracks it the whole way, until it locks into

635
00:34:16,362 --> 00:34:20,999
the stationary bell curve, with variance one half.

636
00:34:21,216 --> 00:34:23,616
Notice what changed in this picture.

637
00:34:23,616 --> 00:34:27,404
We stopped following individual paths, and started watching the density of

638
00:34:27,404 --> 00:34:31,391
the whole cloud evolve, smoothly and deterministically.

639
00:34:31,391 --> 00:34:36,091
That density obeys a partial differential equation of its own, and Itô's lemma

640
00:34:36,091 --> 00:34:37,416
is how we find it.

641
00:34:39,385 --> 00:34:40,985
So here's the question.

642
00:34:40,985 --> 00:34:45,972
If every particle in a cloud follows the same stochastic differential equation,

643
00:34:45,972 --> 00:34:50,260
each with its own independent noise, how does the density of the cloud change

644
00:34:50,260 --> 00:34:52,660
over time?

645
00:34:53,118 --> 00:34:55,755
Itô's lemma answers it, with a classic trick:

646
00:34:55,755 --> 00:34:57,930
don't look at the density directly.

647
00:34:57,930 --> 00:35:01,443
Look at the average of some smooth test function, phi, of X.

648
00:35:01,443 --> 00:35:06,443
By Itô's lemma, phi of X changes by a drift term, mu phi prime plus one half

649
00:35:06,443 --> 00:35:09,930
sigma squared phi double prime, plus a noise term.

650
00:35:09,930 --> 00:35:12,730
Take the average, and the noise term vanishes:

651
00:35:12,730 --> 00:35:15,293
Itô integrals have mean zero.

652
00:35:15,293 --> 00:35:17,905
An average is an integral against the density p.

653
00:35:17,905 --> 00:35:19,780
Now integrate by parts:

654
00:35:19,780 --> 00:35:24,505
once to move the derivative off phi prime, twice for phi double prime.

655
00:35:24,505 --> 00:35:29,318
The boundary terms vanish, and we get phi times something, integrated.

656
00:35:29,318 --> 00:35:34,326
Since this holds for every test function phi, the somethings must be equal.

657
00:35:34,326 --> 00:35:35,226
The result:

658
00:35:35,226 --> 00:35:39,701
the rate of change of the density is minus the slope of mu times p, plus one

659
00:35:39,701 --> 00:35:42,389
half the curvature of sigma squared times p.

660
00:35:42,389 --> 00:35:47,514
This is the Fokker–Planck equation, also called Kolmogorov's forward equation.

661
00:35:47,514 --> 00:35:50,964
The first term carries probability along with the drift.

662
00:35:50,964 --> 00:35:52,776
The second spreads it out.

663
00:35:52,776 --> 00:35:56,701
Randomness in each path became a perfectly deterministic equation for the

664
00:35:56,701 --> 00:35:58,901
cloud.

665
00:35:59,360 --> 00:36:01,022
Start with the simplest case:

666
00:36:01,022 --> 00:36:04,872
pure Brownian motion, no drift, sigma equal to one.

667
00:36:04,872 --> 00:36:07,310
Then Fokker–Planck is the heat equation:

668
00:36:07,310 --> 00:36:10,910
the density changes at a rate equal to one half its curvature.

669
00:36:10,910 --> 00:36:14,747
It's the same equation Einstein derived for diffusing particles.

670
00:36:14,747 --> 00:36:17,997
Here are four thousand particles released at one point.

671
00:36:17,997 --> 00:36:22,535
Their histogram spreads out, exactly along the bell curve with variance t,

672
00:36:22,535 --> 00:36:25,493
which solves the heat equation.

673
00:36:25,493 --> 00:36:27,168
You can see why it spreads.

674
00:36:27,168 --> 00:36:30,730
At the peak, the curve bends down, so the density falls.

675
00:36:30,730 --> 00:36:34,205
Out on the shoulders, it bends up, so the density rises.

676
00:36:34,205 --> 00:36:38,018
Peaks get shaved, valleys get filled, and the bell flattens.

677
00:36:38,018 --> 00:36:42,118
And the width grows like the square root of time.

678
00:36:44,618 --> 00:36:46,155
Now something richer.

679
00:36:46,155 --> 00:36:50,855
Let the drift be the downhill slope of a landscape with two valleys, so particles

680
00:36:50,855 --> 00:36:54,330
roll toward the bottoms, while the noise keeps shaking them.

681
00:36:54,330 --> 00:36:57,801
Drop three thousand particles into the left valley.

682
00:36:57,801 --> 00:37:01,426
At first they huddle in the left valley, and the density there settles into

683
00:37:01,426 --> 00:37:02,376
a bell.

684
00:37:02,376 --> 00:37:07,514
But every so often, a run of unlucky kicks throws a particle over the ridge.

685
00:37:07,514 --> 00:37:09,776
Slowly, the right valley fills.

686
00:37:09,776 --> 00:37:14,014
And all along, the histogram of the simulated particles follows the Fokker–Planck

687
00:37:14,014 --> 00:37:20,501
equation, solved on a computer as a smooth curve, with no randomness at all.

688
00:37:23,193 --> 00:37:25,468
Eventually the cloud stops changing.

689
00:37:25,468 --> 00:37:29,468
Setting the time derivative to zero, and the flow of probability to zero,

690
00:37:29,468 --> 00:37:32,143
gives a first-order equation we can solve:

691
00:37:32,143 --> 00:37:36,268
the stationary density is proportional to e to the minus two V over sigma

692
00:37:36,268 --> 00:37:37,255
squared.

693
00:37:37,255 --> 00:37:41,355
That's exactly Boltzmann's distribution from statistical mechanics, with the

694
00:37:41,355 --> 00:37:44,118
noise strength playing the role of temperature.

695
00:37:44,118 --> 00:37:46,318
Our simulated particles have found it:

696
00:37:46,318 --> 00:37:49,993
by the end, they're split nearly half and half between the valleys.

697
00:37:52,224 --> 00:37:54,461
Fokker–Planck runs forward in time:

698
00:37:54,461 --> 00:37:57,036
given where the particles start, where will they be?

699
00:37:57,036 --> 00:37:58,899
Now turn the question around.

700
00:37:58,899 --> 00:38:00,211
Start a particle at x.

701
00:38:00,211 --> 00:38:03,386
Some payoff g depends on where it ends up at time T.

702
00:38:03,386 --> 00:38:05,861
What is that payoff worth on average?

703
00:38:05,861 --> 00:38:09,849
Call the answer u of t and x.

704
00:38:10,257 --> 00:38:11,969
Here's the simplest case:

705
00:38:11,969 --> 00:38:16,057
a Brownian gambler starts with fortune point three, and plays until reaching

706
00:38:16,057 --> 00:38:19,044
one, a win, or zero, ruin.

707
00:38:19,044 --> 00:38:21,644
Each path is colored by how it ended.

708
00:38:21,644 --> 00:38:27,157
Out of four thousand simulated gamblers, 29.5 percent reach the top first,

709
00:38:27,157 --> 00:38:31,624
and the game lasts 0.203 time units on average.

710
00:38:31,624 --> 00:38:35,336
Both numbers have exact answers, and here's where they come from.

711
00:38:35,336 --> 00:38:37,511
Let u of x be the chance of winning from x.

712
00:38:37,511 --> 00:38:41,574
Your chance, re-evaluated as the game goes on, is a fair game:

713
00:38:41,574 --> 00:38:42,899
it's a martingale.

714
00:38:42,899 --> 00:38:47,674
So by Itô's lemma, its drift, one half u double prime, must be zero.

715
00:38:47,674 --> 00:38:51,099
u is a straight line, zero at zero and one at one:

716
00:38:51,099 --> 00:38:52,374
u of x equals x.

717
00:38:52,374 --> 00:38:53,749
Point three.

718
00:38:53,749 --> 00:38:58,149
The same argument for the expected time gives one half u double prime equals

719
00:38:58,149 --> 00:39:02,011
minus one, and the answer x times one minus x:

720
00:39:02,011 --> 00:39:05,049
0.21.

721
00:39:05,590 --> 00:39:08,115
The same argument works for any payoff.

722
00:39:08,115 --> 00:39:12,003
Your best forecast of the payoff, given everything you know at time s, is

723
00:39:12,003 --> 00:39:13,340
u of s and X s.

724
00:39:13,340 --> 00:39:17,590
As information arrives, the forecast gets updated, but it can't be expected

725
00:39:17,590 --> 00:39:18,578
to drift:

726
00:39:18,578 --> 00:39:21,540
if you expected it to rise, you'd have raised it already.

727
00:39:21,540 --> 00:39:22,903
It's a martingale.

728
00:39:22,903 --> 00:39:26,928
Itô's lemma splits its change into a drift term and a noise term.

729
00:39:26,928 --> 00:39:31,565
A martingale has no drift, so the drift term must vanish, everywhere.

730
00:39:31,565 --> 00:39:36,065
That's a partial differential equation for u, Kolmogorov's backward equation,

731
00:39:36,065 --> 00:39:39,278
solved backward from the payoff at the final time.

732
00:39:39,278 --> 00:39:42,853
Add a discount rate, and you get the Feynman–Kac formula:

733
00:39:42,853 --> 00:39:47,265
averages over random paths solve partial differential equations.

734
00:39:47,265 --> 00:39:52,578
Mark Kac found it in 1949, inspired by Feynman's path integrals in quantum

735
00:39:52,578 --> 00:39:54,965
mechanics.

736
00:39:55,549 --> 00:39:59,786
In two dimensions, this idea solves a classic problem of physics.

737
00:39:59,786 --> 00:40:01,561
Heat the rim of a metal disk:

738
00:40:01,561 --> 00:40:04,149
three hot arcs, three cold ones.

739
00:40:04,149 --> 00:40:06,561
Wait for the temperature inside to settle.

740
00:40:06,561 --> 00:40:09,724
The steady temperature solves Laplace's equation:

741
00:40:09,724 --> 00:40:13,111
zero curvature, on average, in every direction.

742
00:40:13,111 --> 00:40:15,490
What's the temperature at this point?

743
00:40:15,490 --> 00:40:17,328
Brownian motion knows.

744
00:40:17,328 --> 00:40:21,690
Release a random walker from the point, let it wander until it hits the rim,

745
00:40:21,690 --> 00:40:24,040
and record the temperature where it lands.

746
00:40:24,040 --> 00:40:26,003
Do it again, and again.

747
00:40:26,003 --> 00:40:31,290
The average temperature they report converges to the temperature at the point.

748
00:40:32,499 --> 00:40:36,699
With twenty thousand walkers, the average is 0.533.

749
00:40:36,699 --> 00:40:40,174
The exact solution of Laplace's equation at that point:

750
00:40:40,174 --> 00:40:42,336
0.534.

751
00:40:42,336 --> 00:40:44,674
The reason is Itô's lemma once more:

752
00:40:44,674 --> 00:40:48,986
for two-dimensional Brownian motion the drift term is one half the Laplacian,

753
00:40:48,986 --> 00:40:54,299
so a function with zero Laplacian, evaluated along the walk, is a fair game.

754
00:40:54,299 --> 00:40:59,074
Averaging where the walkers stop gives the value where they started.

755
00:40:59,607 --> 00:41:02,182
Now do that at every point of the disk at once.

756
00:41:02,182 --> 00:41:06,119
With one walker per pixel, each pixel is just hot or cold:

757
00:41:06,119 --> 00:41:07,107
pure noise.

758
00:41:07,107 --> 00:41:08,282
Four walkers.

759
00:41:08,282 --> 00:41:09,282
Sixteen.

760
00:41:09,282 --> 00:41:10,269
Sixty-four.

761
00:41:10,269 --> 00:41:11,969
Two hundred fifty-six.

762
00:41:11,969 --> 00:41:15,969
The noise melts into the smooth solution of Laplace's equation.

763
00:41:15,969 --> 00:41:20,019
Shizuo Kakutani discovered this connection in 1944:

764
00:41:20,019 --> 00:41:23,990
harmonic functions are averages over Brownian paths.

765
00:41:23,990 --> 00:41:28,740
So there are two bridges between random paths and partial differential equations.

766
00:41:28,740 --> 00:41:31,903
Fokker–Planck pushes densities forward in time.

767
00:41:31,903 --> 00:41:35,403
Kolmogorov and Feynman–Kac pull averages backward.

768
00:41:35,403 --> 00:41:37,540
Both come straight out of Itô's lemma.

769
00:41:43,117 --> 00:41:46,930
Here's the application that made stochastic calculus famous.

770
00:41:46,930 --> 00:41:50,205
A stock follows geometric Brownian motion.

771
00:41:50,205 --> 00:41:54,242
A call option gives you the right, but not the obligation, to buy the stock

772
00:41:54,242 --> 00:41:56,692
at a fixed price K at a future time T.

773
00:41:56,692 --> 00:41:59,980
If the stock ends above K, you collect the difference;

774
00:41:59,980 --> 00:42:02,255
if not, you walk away with nothing.

775
00:42:02,255 --> 00:42:04,517
What is that right worth today?

776
00:42:04,517 --> 00:42:08,380
Call its value V, a function of time and the stock price.

777
00:42:08,380 --> 00:42:10,805
Itô's lemma tells us how V changes:

778
00:42:10,805 --> 00:42:15,105
a drift part, which includes the curvature term one half sigma squared S squared

779
00:42:15,105 --> 00:42:20,917
V S S, and a noise part, sigma S V S d W.

780
00:42:21,317 --> 00:42:24,680
Black and Scholes, with Robert Merton, had a brilliant idea:

781
00:42:24,680 --> 00:42:25,530
hedge.

782
00:42:25,530 --> 00:42:28,792
Hold the option, and sell delta shares of the stock.

783
00:42:28,792 --> 00:42:33,005
The portfolio's change is the option's change minus delta times the stock's

784
00:42:33,005 --> 00:42:34,017
change.

785
00:42:34,017 --> 00:42:39,300
Both have noise in them, sigma S d W, and both have the drift mu.

786
00:42:39,300 --> 00:42:44,375
Now choose delta to be exactly V S, the option's sensitivity to the stock.

787
00:42:44,375 --> 00:42:46,125
The noise term cancels.

788
00:42:46,125 --> 00:42:47,475
And so does mu:

789
00:42:47,475 --> 00:42:51,013
the stock's expected return has dropped out entirely.

790
00:42:51,013 --> 00:42:53,984
What's left is perfectly predictable.

791
00:42:53,984 --> 00:42:58,084
A riskless portfolio must earn the risk-free interest rate, r, or there'd

792
00:42:58,084 --> 00:42:59,909
be free money to be made.

793
00:42:59,909 --> 00:43:02,096
Set the two equal, and rearrange:

794
00:43:02,096 --> 00:43:08,159
V t plus r S V S, plus one half sigma squared S squared V S S, minus r V,

795
00:43:08,159 --> 00:43:09,446
equals zero.

796
00:43:09,446 --> 00:43:11,484
That's the Black–Scholes equation.

797
00:43:11,484 --> 00:43:16,759
And the term that makes it work, the curvature term, is Itô's correction.

798
00:43:17,217 --> 00:43:19,755
And we know how to solve equations like this one:

799
00:43:19,755 --> 00:43:21,205
Feynman–Kac.

800
00:43:21,205 --> 00:43:25,680
The option's value is the discounted average payoff, computed as if the stock

801
00:43:25,680 --> 00:43:28,530
grew at the risk-free rate r instead of mu.

802
00:43:28,530 --> 00:43:31,717
For a call, the average can be done in closed form:

803
00:43:31,717 --> 00:43:34,484
the famous Black–Scholes formula.

804
00:43:34,484 --> 00:43:38,771
Here's the value as a function of the stock price, a year before expiry, then

805
00:43:38,771 --> 00:43:42,609
closer and closer, until it becomes the hockey-stick payoff.

806
00:43:42,609 --> 00:43:46,459
With a stock at a hundred, a strike of a hundred, five percent interest and

807
00:43:46,459 --> 00:43:51,396
twenty percent volatility, the formula gives 10.4506.

808
00:43:51,396 --> 00:43:57,321
Averaging the payoff over a million simulated risk-neutral paths gives 10.4512,

809
00:43:57,321 --> 00:44:01,759
plus or minus 0.015.

810
00:44:02,317 --> 00:44:04,655
The derivation is also a recipe.

811
00:44:04,655 --> 00:44:07,180
Here's a simulated stock over one year.

812
00:44:07,180 --> 00:44:10,017
Here's what the option is worth along the way.

813
00:44:10,017 --> 00:44:14,305
Now start with the option's price in cash, and each week, hold delta shares

814
00:44:14,305 --> 00:44:16,980
of the stock, financed from the cash.

815
00:44:16,980 --> 00:44:21,605
The portfolio tracks the option, week after week, and at expiry it lands within

816
00:44:21,605 --> 00:44:27,067
a dollar of the payoff, using nothing but the stock and a bank account.

817
00:44:27,617 --> 00:44:28,830
How good is the hedge?

818
00:44:28,830 --> 00:44:33,242
Here's the leftover error after a year, across five thousand simulated years,

819
00:44:33,242 --> 00:44:38,605
rebalancing four times, sixteen, sixty-four, and two hundred fifty-six times.

820
00:44:38,605 --> 00:44:43,767
Each time you rebalance four times as often, the error halves, from 3.25 dollars

821
00:44:43,767 --> 00:44:45,755
down to 43 cents.

822
00:44:45,755 --> 00:44:47,967
In the continuous limit it vanishes:

823
00:44:47,967 --> 00:44:52,367
the option can be manufactured exactly, so its price is forced.

824
00:44:52,367 --> 00:44:57,367
And the leftover error on each step comes from how far delta W squared strays

825
00:44:57,367 --> 00:44:58,830
from delta t:

826
00:44:58,830 --> 00:45:02,842
the very fluctuation that dies away in the quadratic variation.

827
00:45:04,873 --> 00:45:07,385
One last idea, and it's a strange one:

828
00:45:07,385 --> 00:45:09,835
change the probabilities, not the paths.

829
00:45:09,835 --> 00:45:13,673
Here are two thousand Brownian paths, all equally likely.

830
00:45:13,673 --> 00:45:18,973
Now give each path a weight, Z, equal to e to the theta W at time one, minus

831
00:45:18,973 --> 00:45:24,014
one half theta squared, and draw it with opacity proportional to its weight.

832
00:45:24,014 --> 00:45:25,127
Turn up theta.

833
00:45:25,127 --> 00:45:27,202
Paths that end high get heavier;

834
00:45:27,202 --> 00:45:29,602
paths that end low fade away.

835
00:45:29,602 --> 00:45:33,602
The weighted average path tilts up into a straight line of slope theta, and

836
00:45:33,602 --> 00:45:37,652
the weighted distribution of where the paths end slides over to a bell curve

837
00:45:37,652 --> 00:45:39,077
centered at theta.

838
00:45:39,077 --> 00:45:44,002
Under the new weights, W looks exactly like Brownian motion with drift theta.

839
00:45:44,002 --> 00:45:48,239
Turn theta negative, and the drift goes down.

840
00:45:50,689 --> 00:45:52,339
Where does that weight come from?

841
00:45:52,339 --> 00:45:56,377
Compare the bell curve for one step with drift theta to the one without.

842
00:45:56,377 --> 00:46:01,202
Their ratio is e to the theta delta W, minus one half theta squared delta

843
00:46:01,202 --> 00:46:01,839
t.

844
00:46:01,839 --> 00:46:06,639
The steps are independent, so for a whole path the ratios multiply, and the

845
00:46:06,639 --> 00:46:12,481
exponents add up to theta W at time T minus one half theta squared T.

846
00:46:12,481 --> 00:46:16,781
And that minus one half theta squared is Itô's correction yet again.

847
00:46:16,781 --> 00:46:18,956
Apply Itô's lemma to Z:

848
00:46:18,956 --> 00:46:22,906
the minus one half theta squared from the time derivative exactly cancels

849
00:46:22,906 --> 00:46:27,606
the plus one half theta squared from the curvature, so Z has no drift.

850
00:46:27,606 --> 00:46:31,881
It's a martingale, with average one, which makes it a legitimate way to reweight

851
00:46:31,881 --> 00:46:33,614
probabilities.

852
00:46:33,614 --> 00:46:35,402
That's Girsanov's theorem:

853
00:46:35,402 --> 00:46:41,277
reweighting by Z turns W into a Brownian motion with drift theta, or, equivalently,

854
00:46:41,277 --> 00:46:45,239
W minus theta t into a standard Brownian motion.

855
00:46:45,239 --> 00:46:49,764
Changing the drift is the same as changing the odds.

856
00:46:50,214 --> 00:46:53,377
This explains the strangest step in Black–Scholes.

857
00:46:53,377 --> 00:46:56,227
In the real world, the stock drifts at mu.

858
00:46:56,227 --> 00:47:01,289
Reweight with theta equal to r minus mu, over sigma, and under the new probabilities,

859
00:47:01,289 --> 00:47:04,389
the stock drifts at exactly the risk-free rate.

860
00:47:04,389 --> 00:47:08,364
That's the risk-neutral world, where the option price is a plain discounted

861
00:47:08,364 --> 00:47:09,314
average.

862
00:47:09,314 --> 00:47:11,552
It isn't an assumption about investors:

863
00:47:11,552 --> 00:47:14,614
it's Girsanov's theorem.

864
00:47:15,073 --> 00:47:17,960
Changing the odds is also a practical tool.

865
00:47:17,960 --> 00:47:20,785
Suppose you need the probability of a rare event:

866
00:47:20,785 --> 00:47:24,473
say, Brownian motion ending above four at time one.

867
00:47:24,473 --> 00:47:28,160
The exact answer is about three in a hundred thousand.

868
00:47:28,160 --> 00:47:32,373
Simulate a hundred thousand paths, and you'll see it about three times.

869
00:47:32,373 --> 00:47:33,598
We saw it once:

870
00:47:33,598 --> 00:47:36,539
an estimate off by a factor of three.

871
00:47:36,539 --> 00:47:40,639
Instead, simulate with a drift of four, so half the paths land in the rare

872
00:47:40,639 --> 00:47:45,427
region, and undo the change by weighting each path with Girsanov's factor.

873
00:47:45,427 --> 00:47:50,977
Ten thousand tilted paths give 3.24 times ten to the minus five, within about

874
00:47:50,977 --> 00:47:52,639
two percent of the truth.

875
00:47:52,639 --> 00:47:56,789
This is importance sampling, and it's how banks and engineers estimate the

876
00:47:56,789 --> 00:47:58,589
probability of disasters.

877
00:48:00,670 --> 00:48:02,220
Let's put it all together.

878
00:48:02,220 --> 00:48:04,007
It started with one fact:

879
00:48:04,007 --> 00:48:08,157
over a short time d t, Brownian motion moves about root d t.

880
00:48:08,157 --> 00:48:12,232
That makes its paths rough at every scale, with infinite length, but their

881
00:48:12,232 --> 00:48:15,645
squared increments add up to exactly the elapsed time:

882
00:48:15,645 --> 00:48:18,653
d W squared equals d t.

883
00:48:18,653 --> 00:48:21,166
That one rule decides everything else.

884
00:48:21,166 --> 00:48:24,916
Integrals against noise have to be taken at the left end of each step, so

885
00:48:24,916 --> 00:48:29,803
they're fair games, and the integral of W d W picks up a minus one half t.

886
00:48:29,803 --> 00:48:34,353
The chain rule picks up a correction, one half f double prime d t:

887
00:48:34,353 --> 00:48:37,166
noise acting on curvature creates drift.

888
00:48:37,166 --> 00:48:41,970
That's why noisy growth compounds at mu minus one half sigma squared.

889
00:48:41,970 --> 00:48:46,482
Averaging Itô's lemma turns random paths into deterministic equations:

890
00:48:46,482 --> 00:48:51,007
Fokker–Planck pushes densities forward in time, and Kolmogorov and Feynman–Kac

891
00:48:51,007 --> 00:48:56,145
pull averages backward, which is how random walkers solve Laplace's equation.

892
00:48:56,145 --> 00:48:58,682
And in finance it all comes together:

893
00:48:58,682 --> 00:49:02,645
Black and Scholes hedged the noise away to price an option, and Girsanov's

894
00:49:02,645 --> 00:49:07,195
theorem explains why that price is an average in a world where the stock grows

895
00:49:07,195 --> 00:49:09,945
at the risk-free rate.

896
00:49:10,137 --> 00:49:11,887
All of it, from one line.

897
00:49:11,887 --> 00:49:13,687
d W squared equals d t.

898
00:49:13,687 --> 00:49:14,837
Thanks for watching.
